WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:03.790
- Hello, and welcome to
Ed Talks with Khan Academy

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.870
where we talk to influential
people in the education space.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.990
Today, we are happy to welcome Ben Gomes,

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.570
who's the Senior Vice President

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.320
and the Learning &amp; Education
Organization at Google.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.670
Before we get into talking to him,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:22.360
I wanna remind you that we are a nonprofit

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.790
here at Khan Academy.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:27.010
And a lot of our work is possible

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.700
because of donations from folks like you.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.920
So we encourage you if you
are in a place to do so

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.868
to find our sites and

00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:38.330
give what you can to
keep supporting our work.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.870
In addition,

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.460
as the pandemic

00:00:42.572 --> 00:00:43.420
has happened

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.590
we have needed some additional support

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:46.820
to be able to continue

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.810
to provide the education experiences

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:51.190
to students who need them.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:54.300
And we wanna specifically
recognize corporations

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.470
who have provided that support
including Bank of America,

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.010
AT&amp;T, Google.org, Novartis,

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.110
Fastly, and General Motors.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:03.943
Thank you.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.520
In addition to this live video feed

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.580
you can also get an audio version

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.440
of our Ed Talks and Homeroom
with Sal in podcast form

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.770
wherever you find your podcasts,

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.400
look for Homeroom with Sal
and you will find us there.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.400
So with that, I want to
welcome our guests today.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.630
Ben Gomes, as I said,
the Senior Vice President

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.430
of Learning &amp; Education at Google.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.273
Ben, welcome.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.860
- Thank you for having me here, Kristen.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.720
And I also just wanna take the opportunity

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.530
as I saw the intro of
thanking Khan Academy

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.160
for the amazing work you've
been doing for so long

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.223
and especially during the
last year is just wonderful.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:52.900
- Well, thank you.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.890
And we are happy to do it.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.640
Let's start off.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.550
Tell us a little bit about your journey.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.091
How have you come to lead the learning

00:02:01.091 --> 00:02:02.480
and education group at Google?

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.633
What's been your path to get here?

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.830
- You know, if I think
about a lot of my career

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.020
a lot of it has been about
learning and curiosity.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.410
I spent 20 years in search at Google.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.150
And when I thought about
search, a lot of it for me

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.920
was about being able to answer
that curiosity of the moment.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.883
Being able to get that
answer really quickly

00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:26.960
that it stimulates more curiosity
hopefully in the long run.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.220
And after working on search for 20 years,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.930
I wanted to focus on learning more...

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.420
Learning and education more sort

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.190
of broadly across Google's products.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.420
And if you think about Google as a whole,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.620
Google's mission is to organize
the world's information

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.330
and make it universally
accessible and useful.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.640
But a lot of information
is used for learning.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.890
And it's used for changing

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.150
what people can do in the long run.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.690
And a subset of that is
education where it's structured

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.680
and you get a credential at the end of it.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.410
So, there's a lot of
aspects of Google's products

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.360
that can be used for that purpose

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.800
whether it's search or
YouTube or Classroom

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.940
or G Suite and workspace and so on.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.630
That can be used for
learning and education.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.120
I was really interested in that.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.170
And I think it's a core
part of Google's mission.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.920
So I think

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.753
it's a...

00:03:17.753 --> 00:03:21.410
For me it was a really interesting...

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.670
It was following a path that
I've always been interested in

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.460
in some form or the other.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.293
And

00:03:28.518 --> 00:03:30.073
taking it to a deeper level.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:32.323
- Got it.

00:03:32.323 --> 00:03:33.156
Great.

00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:35.210
Tell us a little bit about
your education journey.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.630
Were there any particular teachers

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.770
who were particularly influential to you

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:39.903
and your success?

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:42.783
- Definitely.

00:03:42.783 --> 00:03:43.865
Well, first of all, my mum.

00:03:43.865 --> 00:03:46.883
(chuckles) She was a school teacher

00:03:46.883 --> 00:03:49.620
and she taught geography
which is a subject

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.210
I don't think that taught
in the United States.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.460
But, she was really influential

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.870
in like stimulating curiosity

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.350
because she'd take us out into

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.810
an area and then say,
you know how do you think

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.470
this landscape came to be?

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.070
And it got you thinking about like

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.490
how could this have happened?

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.450
And you may not know the exact answer

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.650
but just the process of
thinking was really great.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.573
And the other teachers in my school,

00:04:14.235 --> 00:04:16.500
there was a priest who
really influence my thinking

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.350
about the importance of
service and serving others.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.540
And there was a chemistry teacher

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.670
who influenced not just me

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.060
but a whole generation
of students in my school

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.660
to go into the sciences.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.750
He was a disciplinarian in a
mold that's not common today

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.410
but his passion for
chemistry was so evident.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.190
And he in got to just so much
to do projects and to play

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.610
that a lot of us came to love chemistry

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.953
and through chemistry to
love science because of that.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.730
- That's great.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.420
So thinking about the field of education

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.990
and the intersection with technology.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.890
Technology is changing

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.700
obviously very quickly but
we've seen some huge advances.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.190
How do you think we can
bring those together?

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.707
What opportunities are
there because of technology

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:10.007
and the improvements in
technology we've seen?

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.063
- Well, I think we're here...

00:05:13.063 --> 00:05:15.840
We need to figure out how to help teachers

00:05:17.373 --> 00:05:19.570
in all kinds of different ways because

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.940
the key things that we
have tried to do in Google

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:28.010
is to try and understand
where we can help teachers

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:30.920
and learners actually

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.080
focus on the learning and
teaching task at hand.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.140
And so that's a core part of

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.810
how we wanna think about the use

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.250
of technology in this space

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.900
as a tool

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.070
to help people whether it's the teachers

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.270
or the students to get what
they're trying to do done.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.110
And so, tools like Classroom

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:54.680
are really structured around trying

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.520
to make the teacher's workflow process

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.000
and the students' work flow process easier

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.100
in giving out assignments and quizzes

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:03.600
and all these sorts of things.

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:07.130
And I think there are other ways

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.910
in which also you can
begin to use technology

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.750
in more complex ways in newer domains.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.340
But the first aspect of
it is trying to figure out

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.640
what do teachers need?

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.150
What is the need of the day

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.250
that we can actually
provide using technology?

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.020
So I think it starts with a
teacher and what they need.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.690
And then it proceeds from there

00:06:27.684 --> 00:06:30.923
to the tools that need to be
built to satisfy their needs.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.679
And I think that's the general framework-

00:06:34.679 --> 00:06:36.440
- (chuckles) that's such a good point.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.480
We often see kind of technology coming in

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:43.370
to tell teachers what they
need as opposed to asking them

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.593
how are you going about
finding out what teachers need?

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.290
- So it is a great, great question.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.780
In an ideal world,
having taken on this role

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.550
I'd have loved to go into into classrooms

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:56.690
and actually ask teachers in person.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.630
But I took on this job
in the middle of COVID

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:00.900
that's not been a possibility.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.240
But we do get a lot of user
feedback from teachers.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.640
We have a lot of forums
with teachers and so on.

00:07:07.988 --> 00:07:09.760
And so we get a lot of feedback

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.530
when you use that really strongly

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.170
to prioritize what we're working on.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.880
Because that feedback from teachers.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.880
We can't satisfy all the
needs that they have.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:20.500
But we prioritize based on

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.850
what they're telling us
is most important to them.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:24.580
What causes them the most problems

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:28.683
and what would help them the
most in solving those problems.

00:07:30.374 --> 00:07:31.691
- That makes sense.

00:07:31.691 --> 00:07:36.190
So what are some of the things
that you see your group there

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:38.360
at Google working on in the future?

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.120
- So there's some very
interesting products that are...

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.670
First of all there's
further generations...

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.620
Further iterations of classroom.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.890
We just announced 15 new features
in a couple of months ago

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.490
that we are still in the
process of rolling out.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.480
Some have rolled out already.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:53.930
Some will be rolled out.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:54.763
But there are many other...

00:07:54.763 --> 00:07:56.650
There are other areas too.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.670
We have an app called Read Along

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:04.080
which is being used to help
children learn to read.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.090
What it does is, it
does speech recognition

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.020
and voice synthesis

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:11.366
so that a child can reading
some text can get some feedback

00:08:11.366 --> 00:08:13.420
on whether you're doing
it correctly or not.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:16.970
And we're working together
with a nonprofit called Pratham

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.960
to actually see whether we do...

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.170
To try and change outcomes

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.850
and literacy levels for children.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.540
And we've been able to see
like even a short time period

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.370
an improvement in literacy

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.880
with this nonprofit working with this tool

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.190
to help improve outcomes.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.230
So I think there's a lot of different ways

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:41.230
in which in the future we
can begin to use technology

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.063
to help

00:08:44.286 --> 00:08:45.886
in deeper ways in the classroom,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.280
in helping the student get feedback

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.180
and in helping the teacher
give out assignments

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.420
and so on in a way that is effective

00:08:58.410 --> 00:08:59.290
is more effective

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.493
than would be possible otherwise.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:05.393
- Yeah.

00:09:05.393 --> 00:09:07.740
So, one of the things that we can think

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.600
about technology doing

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.610
is maybe helping expand the classroom.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.790
Expand out into the world.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.690
Do you think there are
opportunities there?

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:18.523
- Yeah.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.090
The role of technology today

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:22.700
is it has changed...

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.700
What technology is capable of
doing today has changed a lot.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.290
When computers first started,

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.670
they were very good at doing things

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.660
that humans were terrible at doing.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:35.140
Large computations,
complicated mathematics

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.610
which we are terrible at doing.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.500
But they were terrible at doing things

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.490
that we are capable of doing

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:45.060
like understanding that
something is a chair

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.150
that was an unsolved
computer vision problem

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:48.650
for a very long time.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.690
But today technologies beginning

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:53.644
to be able to do things like that.

00:09:53.644 --> 00:09:56.217
So, recently I got interested in botany

00:09:56.217 --> 00:09:58.990
in time of COVID you're
not able to do that much.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:00.280
So I go for walks and-

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.391
- We all get new hobbies, yeah. (chuckles)

00:10:02.391 --> 00:10:03.224
- Yeah.

00:10:03.224 --> 00:10:04.580
I've never been interested in nature

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.750
although I'm in California I'm
not an outdoor kind of person

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:09.900
and I don't much care for exercise.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.460
So the...

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.030
But going on walks I was like, well,

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.150
what's around me is plants and trees.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:16.930
And so I began to be interested in like,

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:18.820
well, what are the types of plants

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:19.880
and trees I'm seeing around me?

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.240
And there's an app on
Google called Google Lens

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.050
that allows you to then
take a picture of a plant

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:27.730
and it'll tell you what it is.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.720
And 10 years ago what I tried this

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.340
or even five years ago this
stuff didn't work really well.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:35.630
And today it works really well.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.500
So you can look at a plant

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:39.390
and begin to figure out what it is

00:10:40.868 --> 00:10:43.570
and then go further from
there into like, okay

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.850
what are the kinds of plants I'm seeing?

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.430
Why are these plants here?

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.440
And so for me it's just
a journey of curiosity

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:54.440
but you can see how
something that is a tool

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:59.420
can help me follow that part
of curiosity that I had.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.340
And so I think there are these connections

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.830
in the real world that
computers are now able to do.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.430
That would be really useful.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:09.200
Another example is our use of AR

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.810
in augmented reality models

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:16.090
that we provided in search
for things like the skeleton

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.660
which allow students visualize a skeleton

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.290
or parts of the body in the
context that they are in

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:25.200
without...

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.920
In a base where they may
not actually have access to

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.825
an actual skeleton in their classroom.

00:11:29.825 --> 00:11:32.590
Which is often a useful teaching aid.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:33.650
So there are many different ways

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:35.560
in which computers are now able

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:37.100
to connect to the real world

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.790
that I think could be of
service in a classroom

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.453
and could be something that
teachers might find useful.

00:11:46.978 --> 00:11:47.811
- That makes sense.

00:11:47.811 --> 00:11:49.410
That makes sense.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:53.280
So, we have a couple
of questions coming in.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.330
We have an interesting question

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:58.463
from Susana Garcia Dominguez on YouTube.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:03.160
She says that Google is the backbone

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.750
of our internet experience.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:06.663
Are people getting smarter?

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:08.483
She wonders?

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:13.660
- You know, It's a really
interesting question.

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:15.350
And I think from my...

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.950
Here's my perspective, when I...

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.820
As I use search, there
are times in the past

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:24.150
when I would hit a wall
in what I needed to know

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:25.630
to take the next step, right?

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:26.760
I'm interested in some area

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.610
the botany example is one example

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:29.970
but many other areas I'm interested in.

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.120
And then I'm interested
in one, some aspect of it.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.670
And the past you'd have to go
to the library, find a book

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:37.860
and actually then read up about it.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.280
And what's the name just didn't do that.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.800
So, but today you can ask that question

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.870
and get an answer quickly and then decide

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.330
if you wanna take that interest further.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:51.400
So to my thinking at
least for me personally,

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.460
it feels to me that allows me

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.150
to develop this kind of
curiosity and exploration

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:58.530
that allows my mind to develop

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.870
in a slightly different
way than it could have.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:03.350
If every time I was
interested in something

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:06.010
it meant finding either the expert in it

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:08.690
or going to a library
to find the right book.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:10.540
That's often the right situation.

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:14.450
Once I've gotten a little bit
of initial interest, right?

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:15.620
The expert will give you something

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.830
that probably a book cannot

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:17.880
and a book will give you something

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.255
that a quick answer want.

00:13:20.255 --> 00:13:22.880
But still there is
something about being able

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.730
to follow those lines
of a curiosity I think

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:31.380
that makes us more curious in the future.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.990
Helps us build that muscle
within us of curiosity.

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:38.340
And I think to me that's
a part of learning I think

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:42.440
that's really the most inspiring,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.650
the most fun and why and
how it can be something

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:47.643
that we pursue all through our lives.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:49.770
- It's interesting.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.490
We had a guest on yesterday,
who's a cognitive scientist.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.620
And we were also talking about curiosity

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.920
and that it can be
powerful but also fragile.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.900
And that we see it in

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.950
young kids so often.

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:08.580
And that then it kind of, you know

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.500
as we get to be adults we
lose some of that wonder.

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:14.040
And he was talking about the fact that

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.360
we are curious about things that we think

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.280
we will help us learn something new

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.490
about something we already know.

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:24.760
And so that strikes me
as related to this idea

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:29.230
of can we use the internet
and the information to

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:33.160
as you said, hey, I know a
little bit about this plant

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.200
and I wanna find out a little bit more

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:38.150
and build that kind of
that piece that I know.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:41.300
So, it seems like that's a powerful idea

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:44.160
that's keeping people
curious as they get older.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:45.740
Is that kind of

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:49.730
maintaining curiosity resonates with you?

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.563
- Very much so.

00:14:50.563 --> 00:14:51.880
And I like that notion of like

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:56.000
there are certain seeds of
curiosity in our everyday lives.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.573
And the question is, will
those seeds find nutrition?

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.870
And we can follow where they take that...

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:06.980
Okay, I'm gonna mix
metaphors terribly here.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:08.223
Where that takes us.

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.330
So, I think

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.380
that sense of wonder also
about the world around us

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:20.380
that sense of the amazingness
of life of nature.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:25.070
All of those things can inspire
things within us in a moment

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:26.020
that can then lead us

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:29.373
on to something that lasts for longer.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:31.940
- That makes sense.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.130
I'm gonna shift gears a little,

