00:25:47.925 --> 00:25:48.925
that widely,

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:54.300
it was not that important an area.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:56.400
I think when I didn't think of it

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:59.420
I don't tend to think of sort of...

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.250
I didn't think of other
people's sacrifices

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:03.790
as being far greater than my own,

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:06.150
the sacrifices sort of
that my parents made

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:08.170
the teachers around me made.

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:11.670
I feel I've been really
fortunate and a beneficiary

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:14.040
of a lot of

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:19.070
what other people around me have done.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:22.760
And I feel the need to serve
others as a result of that.

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:23.640
Because I think

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.970
there's a lot that other people from my...

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.260
Including my parents and so on have done,

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:36.330
I think that I have benefited from

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:41.330
and I think that's a thought
that stays with me quite often.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:43.020
- Yeah, yeah.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:46.270
Tell me a little bit more
about now computer science

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:47.840
of course, is where everyone says,

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.960
oh if you wanna be successful

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:52.870
and be attach to the future,

00:26:52.870 --> 00:26:54.810
go into computer science.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:57.530
But it's interesting that
when you were starting out

00:26:57.530 --> 00:26:58.660
that was not the case.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:01.930
So was it something that you
were just really interested in

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:03.830
or what made you pursue that

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:08.140
even though it wasn't maybe
being as popular then?

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:08.973
- Yeah.

00:27:08.973 --> 00:27:10.810
And when I going...

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:13.060
When I started out, a lot of people were,

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:14.810
there's people who were persuading me

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:18.600
to try and go into one of
the fields like medicine

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:19.707
or law or things of that.
- Sure.

00:27:19.707 --> 00:27:23.410
- But my own interest
was in building things.

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:25.010
I think is some inherent,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.930
inherently there's an engineer in me.

00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:29.453
And so there's that
aspect of building things

00:27:29.453 --> 00:27:31.230
was something that always appealed to me.

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:33.990
And so computer science was something

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:37.250
that inherently just
repealed the first time

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:38.840
I saw a computer.

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:43.720
And so it was something
that drew me very early on.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:47.480
And it's something that the
pleasure of building things,

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:50.800
I think is something of
creating or building something

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:52.960
is something that
doesn't go away from you.

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:56.190
Because I think it can
play a role in your life

00:27:56.190 --> 00:28:01.053
all the way through in small
things or in larger ways.

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:03.210
- Got it.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.970
What advice would you have
for learners that are,

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:08.900
just now kind of coming up

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:12.143
and thinking about their future education

00:28:12.143 --> 00:28:13.683
and their future careers?

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.123
- I think these,

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:18.610
what has served me well, actually

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:21.010
is a breadth of interests.

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:23.700
Because when I started
working, even at Google,

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:28.600
I worked on infrastructure
projects within Google

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.350
on page rank and things like
that, I worked on ranking.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.050
But then I began to be involved

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:37.430
with user interfaces
and things of that sort.

00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:39.700
But what I felt that served me well

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:43.033
was finding out what's
interesting in that area.

00:28:44.472 --> 00:28:46.040
And then being able to pursue it.

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:50.490
So, I would say find the interesting thing

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:53.590
in almost anything that you are learning

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:57.050
and pursue that it gets
to what makes learning,

00:28:57.050 --> 00:29:00.540
I think, interesting and
fun and not just a task.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.850
So that's the piece.

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:03.583
And then when it is interesting,

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.400
when it is more than just a task to do,

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:09.370
I think you get much more out of it

00:29:11.551 --> 00:29:14.563
and not just in the short-term,
but in the long-term.

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:17.390
- Great.

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:20.230
That seems like good
advice for us to end on.

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:22.460
So thank you so much for joining us today.

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:24.173
We appreciate having you.

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:28.043
- Thank you so much
Kristen, for having me here.

00:29:28.043 --> 00:29:28.876
- Great.

00:29:28.876 --> 00:29:30.400
And to all of our guests,

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:31.920
thanks for joining us today.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.240
All of those out there listening

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:34.740
and we will see you next time.

00:29:35.852 --> 00:29:38.435
(bright music)

